{
  "title": "At the doctor's",
  "title_target": "ექიმთან — Ekimtan",
  "goal": "Explain what hurts, name parts of the body, buy medicine at the pharmacy — and meet a verb that builds its sentence the other way around from English.",
  "bekal": [
    "say which part of your body hurts",
    "name common symptoms: fever, cough, nausea",
    "ask for medicine at the pharmacy",
    "mention allergies and medicine you take regularly",
    "ask for help in an emergency"
  ],
  "dialogs": [
    {
      "title": "დიალოგი — A throat that isn't right",
      "intro": "Ani gets sick in her fourth week of winter and goes to a clinic. Notice the verb \"hurt\" — the sentence is built the other way around, and that's how Georgian puts the person who suffers in the picture.",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "ექიმი:",
          "text": "[გამარჯობათ|Ga-mar-jo-bat]. [რა|Ra] [გჭირთ|gch'irt]?",
          "id": "Good afternoon. What's the problem?",
          "literal": "hello-polite. what — bothers-you?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "ანი:",
          "text": "[ყელი|Q'e-li] [მტკივა|mt'k'i-va] [და|da] [თავიც|ta-vits] [მტკივა|mt'k'i-va].",
          "id": "My throat hurts and my head hurts too.",
          "literal": "throat — hurts-to-me — and — head-too — hurts-to-me."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "ექიმი:",
          "text": "[სიცხე|Sits-khe] [გაქვთ|gakvt]?",
          "id": "Do you have a fever?",
          "literal": "temperature — you-have?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "ანი:",
          "text": "[დილით|Di-lit] [ოცდათვრამეტი|ots-dat-vra-me-t'i] [იყო|i-q'o]. [ხველაც|Khve-lats] [მაქვს|makvs].",
          "id": "It was 38 this morning. I have a cough too.",
          "literal": "morning-by — thirty-eight — was. cough-too — I-have."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "ექიმი:",
          "text": "[რამდენი|Ram-de-ni] [დღეა|dghe-a]?",
          "id": "How many days has it been?",
          "literal": "how-many — day-is?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "ანი:",
          "text": "[სამი|Sa-mi] [დღეა|dghe-a]. [ღამით|Gha-mit] [უარესია|u-a-re-si-a].",
          "id": "Three days. It's worse at night.",
          "literal": "three — day-is. night-by — worse-is."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "ექიმი:",
          "text": "[პირი|P'i-ri] [გააღეთ|ga-a-ghet]… [კარგი|K'ar-gi]. [ვირუსია|Vi-ru-si-a], [სერიოზული|se-ri-o-zu-li] [არაფერი|a-ra-pe-ri].",
          "id": "Open your mouth… Okay. It's a virus, nothing serious.",
          "literal": "mouth — open… good. virus-is, serious — nothing."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "ანი:",
          "text": "[წამალი|Ts'a-ma-li] [მჭირდება|mch'ir-de-ba]?",
          "id": "Do I need medicine?",
          "literal": "medicine — I-need?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "ექიმი:",
          "text": "[ეს|Es] [სამი|sa-mi] [დღე|dghe] [დალიეთ|da-li-et]. [ბევრი|Bev-ri] [თბილი|tbi-li] [წყალი|ts'q'a-li] [დალიეთ|da-li-et] [და|da] [დაისვენეთ|da-is-ve-net].",
          "id": "Take this for three days. Drink plenty of warm water and rest.",
          "literal": "this — three — day — drink. much — warm — water — drink — and — rest."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "ანი:",
          "text": "[ალერგია|A-ler-gi-a] [მაქვს|makvs] [პენიცილინზე|p'e-ni-tsi-lin-ze].",
          "id": "I'm allergic to penicillin.",
          "literal": "allergy — I-have — penicillin-on."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "ექიმი:",
          "text": "[კარგია|K'ar-gi-a] [რომ|rom] [თქვით|tkvit]. [ეს|Es] [ანტიბიოტიკი|an-t'i-bi-o-t'i-k'i] [არ|ar] [არის|a-ris].",
          "id": "Good that you told me. This isn't an antibiotic.",
          "literal": "good-is — that — you-said. this — antibiotic — not — is."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "ანი:",
          "text": "[გმადლობთ|Gmad-lobt], [ექიმო|e-ki-mo]. [რამდენი|Ram-de-ni] [გადავიხადო|ga-da-vi-kha-do]?",
          "id": "Thank you, doctor. How much do I owe?",
          "literal": "thank-you, doctor-calling. how-much — I-must-pay?"
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        "⚠️ **მტკივა builds its sentence the other way around.** *თავი მტკივა* literally means \"head hurts-to-me\" — the subject of the sentence is **the body part**, not the person. That's why there's no ვ- in front, and no need for a word for \"I\". The forms: **მ**ტკივა (hurts me), **გ**ტკივა (hurts you), **ს**ტკივა (hurts him/her).",
        "**When more than one part hurts**, *თავი და ყელი მტკივა* is still correct: the verb could go plural, but in conversation Georgian usually keeps *მტკივა*.",
        "**რა გჭირს? / რა გჭირთ? = \"what's the problem?\"** Literally \"what's troubling you?\". Every doctor's standard opening line. You already know a relative: მჭირდება (I need) from unit 7.",
        "**სიცხე გაქვთ? — a fever is something you \"have\", not something you \"are\".** *სიცხე მაქვს* (I have a fever), *ხველა მაქვს* (I have a cough), *სურდო მაქვს* (I have a cold). This pattern works for almost every symptom, and you've known it since unit 4.",
        "**Body temperature is given in Celsius, without a decimal**: ოცდათვრამეტი (38), ოცდაცხრამეტი (39). Notice that the twenties system from unit 6 comes back at the least convenient moment to do math.",
        "**ექიმო — notice the -ო ending.** That's the form for calling someone (the vocative): ექიმ**ო** (Doctor!), ბატონ**ო** (Sir!), ქალბატონ**ო** (Ma'am!). The -ი ending turns into -ო whenever you address someone directly.",
        "**ბატონო and ქალბატონო are polite forms of address you must know.** ბატონო for men, ქალბატონო for women — used with doctors, officials, older people, and anyone you respect. ბატონო is also often used on its own as a polite reply when someone calls you, like \"yes, sir?\"."
      ],
